! Windows 11 + WSL2 developer host

This runbook prepares a Windows 11 machine and one WSL2 Linux distro for the Lararium repository. It holds a bounded host profile: WSL resource limits, systemd, an early memory guard, a small swap policy, a Python venv, and project tooling. It does not turn a laptop into a production Lararium, change Docker's security policy, delete another distro, or make a network identity claim.

''Who this serves:'' a household standing a lararium on its shared Windows 11 PC, with one person comfortable at a terminal, as much as a developer on a workstation. Windows 11 Home qualifies. Every step reads what the machine already holds and reports `needs-you` for the few choices only a person can make.

The scripts support maintained apt-based WSL distributions. A non-apt distro remains a valid WSL vessel; install equivalent packages there and use the read-only witness as the contract. The `-Distro` PowerShell parameter names a particular distro when needed. When absent, the script selects the registered default non-Docker distro. It never assumes Ubuntu, a drive letter, a Windows account name, host RAM, or processor count.

Windows-side steps run in PowerShell. Linux-side steps run inside the selected WSL distro. `.wslconfig` applies only after `wsl --shutdown`, so reserve that command for a session boundary.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/preflight>>

!! 1 · Preconditions — Windows

!!! Edition, virtualization, one account

* ''Edition.'' WSL 2 runs on every desktop edition of Windows 11, Home included; it ships as the "Virtual Machine Platform" optional component, a subset of Hyper-V that needs no Hyper-V role. Nothing in this runbook requires Pro.
* ''Virtualization.'' The CPU's virtualization extension (Intel VT-x, AMD-V, often labelled SVM) must stand enabled in UEFI/BIOS setup. Check first: Task Manager → Performance → CPU reads `Virtualization: Enabled`. When it reads `Disabled`, restart into firmware setup (Settings → System → Recovery → Advanced startup, or the maker's boot key) and turn it on; the setting usually sits under CPU or Advanced. Error `0x80370102` at distro launch names this exact gap.
* ''One Windows account.'' WSL registers each distro, and reads `%UserProfile%\.wslconfig`, under the Windows account that created it; another account on the same PC sees no distro and no config. A household picks one Windows account to own the lararium and runs every step of this runbook signed in as that account. A second account starts from nothing.
* ''Space on C:.'' WSL stores distros on the system drive. Error `0x80070003` at install means "where new content is saved" points elsewhere.

!!! Install or update WSL

Open a terminal ''as Administrator'' for the first line only: right-click the Start button → ''Terminal (Admin)''. A restart follows it; the remaining lines run in an ordinary terminal. Then choose a distribution listed by the current Windows build:

```powershell
wsl --install --no-distribution                         # Administrator; enables Virtual Machine Platform; restart after
wsl --update                                            # moves inbox WSL to the Store build; repeat to stay current
wsl --version                                           # 2.5.6 or later; when this prints the help text instead, inbox WSL answered: run wsl --update
wsl --list --online
wsl --install -d <DistroName>                           # Ubuntu-24.04 fits an apt-based stand; it asks you to invent a Linux user name and password
wsl --list --verbose
winget install --id Microsoft.PowerShell --source winget   # PowerShell 7 (pwsh), the runbook engine
```

PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) runs the Windows stand. Windows PowerShell 5.1 ships with Windows and stays as the bootstrap: `stand-windows.ps1` reads its own engine first and, under 5.1 with `pwsh` installed, relaunches itself there once; without `pwsh` it reports `needs-you` and continues degraded, since every later step tolerates 5.1. The script keeps a UTF-8 BOM and ASCII-only code so the 5.1 bootstrap parse holds. `witness.sh` and the Linux stand read for `pwsh` by absolute path and report its absence.

Complete the distro's first-launch user creation before running the Linux script: the Linux user name and password live inside the distro and bear no relation to the Windows account; typed passwords show nothing on screen.

!!! Containers: Docker Engine in the distro

The lararium kit runs in a container behind this WSL2 instance. Ubuntu's own `docker.io` package inside the distro supplies that, runs on Windows Home, costs nothing, and needs no Windows-side program; `systemd=true` (section 4) supervises its daemon, so install it after the first Linux stand and its `wsl --shutdown`. The Linux stand reports the exact install line as `needs-you`. The engine runs rootful: WSL 2.9+ breaks rootless Docker and Podman (microsoft/WSL #41492), so the `docker` group on this host reads root-equivalent; say so to whoever joins it.

Microsoft's own WSL containers (`wslc`, GA in WSL 3.0.1) run single containers on Home with nothing to install, but carry no compose and no Docker socket yet, so the stack stays on the in-distro engine. Fallbacks, the Docker Desktop nuance, and the tripwire that moves the stack onto `wslc` live in [[lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/containers]]. Docker Desktop's own `docker-desktop` distributions, when present, are expected and never setup-script targets.

Keep repositories under the distro's ext4 filesystem, such as `~/src/Synthetic-Dream-Machine`, rather than `/mnt/c`. The mounted Windows filesystem remains useful for interchange; it is not the home for package installs, file-watch-heavy tests, or Git working trees.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/scripts>>

!! 2 · Idempotent stands

Each script reads the current state, writes only a declared difference, and labels the result `already`, `set`, `would-set`, or `needs-you`. A second run changes nothing when its owned intent already holds. No script unregisters, exports, moves, or chooses between distributions. The Linux stand asks for your Linux password once (`sudo`); `--dry-run` asks for nothing.

```sh
# Once, inside the distro: fetch the repository onto its ext4 disk.
sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y git
git clone <repository-url> ~/src/Synthetic-Dream-Machine
cd ~/src/Synthetic-Dream-Machine

# Inside the checked-out repository, in WSL:
tools/wsl2/stand-linux.sh --dry-run
tools/wsl2/stand-linux.sh
tools/wsl2/stand-linux.sh --with-project       # explicit pnpm workspace install

# From inside WSL, same directory. Full paths, because the stand takes Windows tools off $PATH:
"/mnt/c/Program Files/PowerShell/7/pwsh.exe" -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/wsl2/stand-windows.ps1 -DryRun
"/mnt/c/Program Files/PowerShell/7/pwsh.exe" -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/wsl2/stand-windows.ps1
# Without PowerShell 7 yet, Windows PowerShell 5.1 bootstraps the same file (and relaunches under pwsh once installed):
/mnt/c/Windows/System32/WindowsPowerShell/v1.0/powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/wsl2/stand-windows.ps1 -DryRun
# From a Windows terminal instead, cd to \\wsl.localhost\<DistroName>\home\<linux-user>\src\Synthetic-Dream-Machine first:
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/wsl2/stand-windows.ps1 -Distro <DistroName>   # omit -Distro for the registered default
pwsh -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools/wsl2/stand-windows.ps1 -Sparse                # opt-in only; see section 3

tools/wsl2/witness.sh                          # writes nothing; nonzero means drift
```

The Linux stand installs only ordinary apt packages, writes `/etc/wsl.conf`, configures `earlyoom` and `vm.swappiness`, maintains `~/.venv` from `requirements.txt`, and checks Node/pnpm and Docker Engine. It reports `needs-you` with the exact commands when Node 24+ or Docker is absent rather than running a third-party installer on the operator's behalf. `--with-project` explicitly runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.

The Windows stand reads the host first without elevation (edition, Windows account, hypervisor and firmware virtualization state, WSL engine version) and reports each gap as `needs-you`. It then merges only `memory`, `swap`, `processors`, and `autoMemoryReclaim`, plus `sparseVhd` when `-Sparse` opts in. It preserves other `.wslconfig` content, including network/VPN settings and comments. It inspects other registered distros but leaves them untouched.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/resource-profile>>

!! 3 · Bounded VM profile — Windows

The Windows script derives these values from the machine it reads:

```ini
[wsl2]
memory=<five-eighths of host RAM, at least 2 GB>
swap=4GB
processors=<logical processors minus two, at least two>

[experimental]
autoMemoryReclaim=gradual
```

Microsoft's defaults, for comparison: `memory` at 50% of host RAM, `swap` at 25% of that rounded up to a GB, `processors` equal to the host's logical count. The profile leaves capacity for Windows while giving the Linux toolchain a useful ceiling. It is a developer-host default, not a universal performance law: an operator may choose a different bounded profile for a low-memory laptop, a shared workstation, or a dedicated build host. The script only owns the keys above.

''Sparse VHD stays opt-in.'' Since WSL 2.5.6 (April 2025) Microsoft gates sparse virtual disks behind `--allow-unsafe`; `wsl --manage <Distro> --set-sparse true` answers "sparse VHD support is currently disabled due to potential data corruption". The script therefore writes `sparseVhd` and runs `--set-sparse true --allow-unsafe` only under `-Sparse`, and names the warning when it does. An existing sparse disk stays as found. To return deleted space to Windows without that risk: `wsl --shutdown`, then in an Administrator prompt `diskpart` → `select vdisk file="<path to ext4.vhdx>"` → `compact vdisk`. The script prints the vhdx path; `Optimize-VHD` needs Hyper-V tooling absent on Home, so `diskpart` serves every edition. When your `wsl --help` lists `--compact` under `--manage`, `wsl --manage <Distro> --compact` does the same job without diskpart.

Network mode, DNS tunneling, proxy integration, firewall choices, Defender exclusions, and WSL idle policy depend on local security and network conditions. This baseline deliberately does not set them. If an organization permits an antivirus exclusion for a WSL virtual disk, make that a separately reviewed local policy; never infer it from this runbook.

Run `wsl --shutdown` from Windows after a `.wslconfig` change, when no shell, daemon, agent, container, or test needs the VM to remain alive.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/linux-profile>>

!! 4 · Linux profile — systemd and memory recovery

The Linux script maintains this narrow `/etc/wsl.conf` intent:

```ini
[boot]
systemd=true

[user]
default=<the Linux user who ran the stand>

[interop]
enabled=true
appendWindowsPath=false
```

With inherited Windows PATH entries disabled, invoke the few Windows tools needed from WSL by absolute path or add deliberate local aliases. This keeps slow mounted paths out of ordinary command lookup. `systemd=true` supplies service supervision and per-job scopes.

`earlyoom` receives `-m 5 -s 50`: it acts only when available memory falls below five percent and swap usage reaches fifty percent. `vm.swappiness=10` retains swap as a short recovery buffer. The combination protects interactive use from a runaway native compiler, parser, test, or WebAssembly process without pretending that a VM resource limit alone supplies graceful recovery.

The memory-guard reasoning and the historical incident remain at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/memory-guard">> and <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/wsl2/incident-2026-09-18">>. They explain the pattern; they do not prescribe one operator's former machine layout.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/project>>

!! 5 · Repository tools — Linux

Run these in the clone from section 2, after the first `tools/wsl2/stand-linux.sh` and the `wsl --shutdown` it asks for, so `systemd` already runs when Docker's daemon installs.

```sh
cd ~/src/Synthetic-Dream-Machine

# Node.js 24 via nvm — Microsoft's WSL guidance; per-user, no sudo, no third-party apt repo.
# Ubuntu's apt `nodejs` lags far behind 24. Node 24 bundles Corepack (Node 25+ dropped it): stay on 24.
curl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.8/install.sh | bash
source ~/.nvm/nvm.sh && nvm install 24 && nvm alias default 24

# Docker Engine from Ubuntu — the container host for the lararium kit; open a new terminal after.
sudo apt update && sudo apt install docker.io docker-compose-v2 && sudo usermod -aG docker "$USER"

tools/wsl2/stand-linux.sh --with-project
```

Open a new terminal tab between the Docker line and the stand: group membership and the nvm shell hook both land at the next login. The Linux stand reads for Node 24+, Corepack, and a reachable Docker daemon and prints these same lines as `needs-you` while any one stays absent; it runs none of them for you. The stand's own `corepack enable` exposes `pnpm`; the first `pnpm` command then fetches pnpm 10, which satisfies the repository's `engines` (pnpm 9 or later).

The script creates one `~/.venv` and records the digest of `requirements.txt` only after a successful install. A changed requirements file causes the next stand to repair that venv. The workspace install stays opt-in because it may fetch packages and materialize a large `node_modules`; its lockfile makes the intended dependency graph explicit.

For individual native or resource-heavy commands, retain a local resource boundary:

```sh
ulimit -v 4000000; timeout 120 <native-command>
systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=8G <node-or-wasm-command>
```

Use the first form for a process that respects a virtual-address cap. Prefer the systemd scope for Node or WebAssembly, whose virtual reservation can make `ulimit -v` misleading.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/verify>>

!! 6 · Verify and repair

```sh
tools/wsl2/witness.sh
free -h
nproc
systemctl is-active earlyoom
```

A witness drift states the local condition it could not verify. Repair only that declared condition, then re-run it. A witness is an observation from this distro's causal island: it does not assert that another Windows host, another distro, or a Docker mesh shares the same state.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/troubleshooting>>

!! 7 · The six errors a household meets

|! you see |! it means |! do this |
| `WslRegisterDistribution failed with error: 0x80370102` or `HCS_E_HYPERV_NOT_INSTALLED` | the hypervisor cannot start: firmware virtualization off, or Virtual Machine Platform absent | Task Manager → Performance → CPU: when `Virtualization: Disabled`, enable VT-x / AMD-V (SVM) in UEFI setup. Then, as Administrator: `wsl --install --no-distribution`, restart. If it persists, `bcdedit /enum | findstr -i hypervisorlaunchtype` should read `Auto`; set it with `bcdedit /set hypervisorlaunchtype Auto` and restart |
| `Installation failed with error 0x80070003` | WSL installs only on the system drive; Microsoft lists this code for firmware virtualization off as well | Settings → System → Storage → Advanced storage settings → Where new content is saved → set new apps to `C:` and retry; still failing, follow the row above |
| `wsl --version` prints the whole help text; `systemd` never starts; `wsl --manage` unknown | the inbox WSL that shipped with Windows, not the Store build | `wsl --update` (open the Microsoft Store if it asks), then `wsl --shutdown` and reopen the distro |
| `'wsl' is not recognized as the name of a cmdlet` | no WSL on this PC yet | as Administrator: `wsl --install --no-distribution`, restart, then section 1 from the top |
| `Temporary failure in name resolution` inside the distro | DNS stopped reaching the Windows side, usually after a network change, sleep, or a VPN coming up | `wsl --shutdown` from PowerShell, wait eight seconds, reopen. A home router needs nothing more; a VPN or third-party firewall that keeps breaking it belongs to the network choices this runbook leaves to the operator |
| `apt`, `git`, or `pnpm` complain about certificates or dates; `date` inside WSL lags Windows | the VM clock stopped while the PC slept or hibernated | `sudo hwclock -s` inside the distro, or `wsl --shutdown` and reopen. Windows Fast Startup counts as a hibernate for this purpose |

Two shapes a household also meets that carry no error text: the Linux password prompt shows nothing while typing, and a distro installed by one Windows account stays invisible to another. Both read as intended.

<<~/ahu>>

